Subject: Dedicated threaded handlers for seven cold opcodes

What this does
  Dedicated threaded handlers for seven cold opcodes.

Why we need it
  `branch_cmp_loc_reg`, `special_object`, `make_closure`, `set_name`, `get_arg_field`, `put_arg_xfer` and `put_local_s1` each dispatched through `regvm_t_cold` -> `regvm_t_cold_slow` -> `regvm_exec_one`, a call and a second switch on top of the opcode. The `unsafe[]` test in `regvm_code_light_safe` keys on `regvm_threaded[op] == regvm_t_cold`, so giving them handlers would have widened light-frame eligibility; five read record-only frame fields (`sf`, `argv`, `arg_buf`, `arg_owned`, `new_target`) and the frame has only the 9-slot light prefix, so the corpus segfaulted.

What it adds
  Each is its own `regvm_t_<name>` handler, built from that opcode's `regvm_exec_one` arm (record field names already) with operands decoded from `regvm_rop_fmt`; `special_object`, `make_closure` and `set_name` keep a `noinline` `_slow` half so the dispatch-path handler stays frameless. `QJS_REGVM_FAST_COLD=0` restores the old table entries, and under it `regvm_code_light_safe` forces all seven `unsafe[]` so no function's frame kind changes: light-eligible and record-eligible function counts over the corpus.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
